Id: stor006w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
References: crypttab(5)
Controls: CIS 3.11; NIST SC-28; ISO 8.24

The root file system is encrypted, but swap is not: a swap partition
on a plain device, or a swap file on an unencrypted file system.
Whatever the kernel swaps out of memory, which can include passwords,
keys and the contents of encrypted files, is written to the disk in
clear and stays there, so the encryption of the rest can be read
around.

To fix it, put swap on the encrypted volume (a swap file on the
encrypted root, or a logical volume inside the LUKS container), or
encrypt the swap partition with a random key at each boot (a crypttab
line with /dev/urandom as the key and the swap option), which rules out
hibernation.
